WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.721 --> 00:00:16.202
wasn't able to help Jenny with her school problems.

2
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:20.765
But I had a new invention idea, and it was going to be great.

3
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:26.909
This invention wouldn't fall into a fish tank or get caught on anyone's sweater.

4
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.212
And there was still time to write about it for Mr.

5
00:00:30.272 --> 00:00:33.265
Bright. "What's your idea, Iggy?"

6
00:00:33.745 --> 00:00:35.386
asked Jenny as we headed home.

7
00:00:35.406 --> 00:00:39.667
"Did you see the way that ticket machine worked at the bakery?"

8
00:00:40.027 --> 00:00:40.488
I asked.

9
00:00:40.508 --> 00:00:45.390
"You mean where you pick a number so you can save your place in line?"

10
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:46.470
asked Molly.

11
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.911
"That's right," I said.

12
00:00:48.931 --> 00:00:52.172
"We could print tickets for the bumpy slide.

13
00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:55.874
Kids would pick a ticket and wait for their number to be called."

14
00:00:57.074 --> 00:01:01.056
"No one would have to stand in line anymore," said Jenny.

15
00:01:02.256 --> 00:01:02.796
I nodded.

16
00:01:02.816 --> 00:01:06.678
"Kids could run around the playground until their number was called."

17
00:01:06.698 --> 00:01:11.521
"Your invention is just in time, Iggy," said Josh.

18
00:01:11.541 --> 00:01:13.742
"I heard Mr.

19
00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:17.164
Dalmati say that kids were pushing and shoving too much in line.

20
00:01:17.504 --> 00:01:19.845
And no one gets much exercise at recess anymore."

21
00:01:21.126 --> 00:01:22.347
I frowned, thinking.

22
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:28.270
"I could cut up pieces of cardboard and print the numbers on them," I said.

23
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.492
"Then kids can pick the numbers out of a bag."

24
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.263
Everyone agreed to help print tickets for the slide.

25
00:01:37.684 --> 00:01:41.007
So we went upstairs to my apartment to get some cardboard.

26
00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:45.531
I found some that I had used for a project in school.

27
00:01:47.893 --> 00:01:49.614
Then we went to Molly's apartment.

28
00:01:51.536 --> 00:01:56.760
Molly and Caswell's babysitter, Lucy, had just made some chocolate chip cookies.

29
00:01:57.981 --> 00:02:00.924
The apartment smelled almost as good as Mom's Bakery!

30
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.402
So we all sat down and had warm cookies and lemonade.

31
00:02:09.003 --> 00:02:10.444
After that we got to work.

32
00:02:12.905 --> 00:02:15.307
We cut the cardboard into small squares.

33
00:02:17.648 --> 00:02:21.530
Then we printed big numbers on each square with a magic marker.

34
00:02:24.272 --> 00:02:27.874
We printed numbers all the way up to one hundred.

35
00:02:29.255 --> 00:02:31.356
Everyone helped, even Caswell.

36
00:02:32.348 --> 00:02:36.831
Now during recess kids could pick a number to take a turn on the slide.

37
00:02:36.851 --> 00:02:40.313
"We will need a number caller," I said.

38
00:02:40.333 --> 00:02:42.614
"What's a number caller?"

39
00:02:43.054 --> 00:02:43.875
asked Jenny.

40
00:02:43.895 --> 00:02:48.577
"It's the person who calls out the next number," I explained.

41
00:02:48.597 --> 00:02:51.659
"Remember the man at Mom's Bakery?"

42
00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:54.781
Molly grinned and imitated him.

43
00:02:54.801 --> 00:02:57.462
"Number seven is next!"

44
00:02:57.482 --> 00:03:00.644
"Exactly," I said.

45
00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:05.389
"Let's take turns calling out the numbers," said Jenny.

46
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.631
I wanted this to be a really, really good invention.

47
00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:12.892
So I came up with one more idea.

48
00:03:12.912 --> 00:03:17.434
"I'll ask my mom if she'll take us to Dollar Delight," I said.

49
00:03:17.454 --> 00:03:20.435
"They sell lots of cheap little toys.

50
00:03:21.836 --> 00:03:24.877
Kids can pick a number from one bag and a toy from another.

51
00:03:26.558 --> 00:03:28.659
Then they can have fun while they're waiting."

52
00:03:28.679 --> 00:03:31.300
"But won't we run out of toys?"

53
00:03:31.856 --> 00:03:32.677
asked Jenny.

54
00:03:32.697 --> 00:03:34.918
"No," I said.

55
00:03:34.938 --> 00:03:39.821
"When the kids turn in their tickets, they'll turn in their toys too."

56
00:03:39.841 --> 00:03:43.163
"Wow," said Molly.

57
00:03:43.183 --> 00:03:46.024
"This is a really great invention, Iggy."

58
00:03:47.205 --> 00:03:48.646
I hoped Molly was right.

59
00:03:49.686 --> 00:03:55.250
My Fabulous Glasses-Finder had sounded like a good idea too—at first.

60
00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.092
But my Pick-a-Number invention was different.

61
00:04:00.356 --> 00:04:02.378
It would solve a big problem at school.

62
00:04:03.559 --> 00:04:05.781
Maybe it would even be in Mr.

63
00:04:05.841 --> 00:04:06.882
Bright's new book!

64
00:04:08.804 --> 00:04:11.226
After dinner my mom took us to Dollar Delight.

65
00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.632
We bought lots of little toys for only about ten dollars.

66
00:04:20.113 --> 00:04:22.556
We also bought two colorful shopping bags.

67
00:04:23.616 --> 00:04:27.340
We planned to fill one shopping bag with tickets and the other with toys.

68
00:04:29.862 --> 00:04:32.961
I could hardly wait until recess the next day!
